      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 63-year-old man with non-ST-elevation acute myocardial infarction had undergone PROMUS Element stent implantation for stenosis with thrombus in the left main trunk and the proximal left anterior descending artery. A 6-month follow-up angiography revealed no restenosis or stent thrombosis, but severe provoked spasms in three vessels, for which medications were administered. Three years later, the patient experienced new-onset rest angina one morning. Optical coherence tomography revealed subclinical very late stent thromboses inside and outside the stent. The patients experienced a severe spasm. Platelet function was adequately reduced, and the cytochrome P450 2C19 genotype indicated an extensive metabolizer phenotype. The spasm may have caused the thromboses."},
